evision10_review_transport.py:22:CLI = load_script(\"forge_cli_revision10_review_transport_tests\", CLI_PATH)\ntests/test_revision10_review_transport.py:25:FIXTURE_SUPPORT = load_script(\ntests/fixtures/prose-and-position-journal.jsonl:38:{\"basis\":[],\"check\":\"git status --short internal/world/dbgen; git diff internal/world/queries/save.sql\",\"criterion\":\"gate-2: generated code drift — make sqlcgen then git diff --exit-code internal/world/dbgen must be clean\",\"id\":\"check-17\",\"method\":\"inspection\",\"observation\":\"CONFIRMED unmet. internal/world/queries/save.sql changed materially — ListRenderedTexts now inner-joins document_opens, DeleteRenderedTexts became conditional on NOT EXISTS in document_opens, and three queries are new (ListRenderedTextCaches, ListDocumentOpens, InsertDocumentOpen with ON CONFLICT (copy_id) DO NOTHING) — while internal/world/dbgen shows no modification at all. sqlc-generated bindings for the new and changed queries are therefore absent, so the drift gate cannot pass and the sqlite layer cannot be compiling against regenerated bindings. The implementer was blocked from make sqlcgen by network restrictions on the pinned sqlc module and disclosed this plainly. Remediation: orchestrator or fix execution runs make sqlcgen (the orchestrator has proven network access for pinned module downloads — make typegen succeeded earlier in this run) and then requires git diff --exit-code internal/world/dbgen to be clean.\",\"result\":\"failed\",\"task\":\"task-04\",\"type\":\"verification\"}\ntests/fixtures/prose-and-position-journal.jsonl:55:{\"basis\":[],\"finding\":\"Every Codex implementer in this run (codex-impl-02, -03, -04) was unable to run a single Go gate. Diagnosed by experiment, not inference: the -s workspace-write sandbox permits writes only inside the workspace, so GOCACHE (/home/agents/.cache/go-build) is denied and Go reports the policy denial as 'read-only file system'. A control probe with identical flags reproduced it exactly; the same probe plus --add-dir for GOCACHE and GOMODCACHE returned BUILD_EXIT:0 and VET_EXIT:0. A second, causally linked environment fault was also found: ~3.1G of abandoned Go caches in /tmp, created by agents in other sessions redirecting caches there to escape this same restriction, had filled the 7.9G tmpfs and produced spurious 'database or disk is full (13)' failures (check-22).\",\"id\":\"decision-11\",\"outcome\":\"user_direction\",\"resolution\":\"Sandbox broadening APPROVED by the user (2026-08-18, this session) after the orchestrator surfaced it as control-class and declined to apply it unilaterally, per the rule that model, effort and sandbox values are control-class and a broader sandbox must never be silently substituted. Future implementer and reviewer launches add three writable roots and nothing else: --add-dir /home/agents/.cache/go-build (GOCACHE), --add-dir /home/agents/go/pkg/mod (GOMODCACHE), and --add-dir /home/agents/.cache/palimpsest-tmp (the disk-backed TMPDIR). The third is required and was missed in the first pass of this analysis: codex-impl-04 was told to set TMPDIR to that path and was denied there too, so the orchestrator had handed it a second blocked directory. Scope of the grant is deliberately minimal — all three are build/temp caches, not source. No network access is granted: make sqlcgen was proven to work fully offline from the already-populated module cache via GOPROXY=file:///home/agents/go/pkg/mod/cache/download with GOSUMDB=off (exit 0, dbgen idempotent), so the deprecation-lookup failure that blocked codex-impl-02 needs no network. No writes outside those three caches, no repository outside the worktree, no credentials. Justification recorded for review: the status quo had three implementers author roughly 2000 lines of Go each without compiling once, which is how a latent P-PROJ-1 disclosure defect (the derived passage anchor at check-20) was written and survived to the orchestrator's gate rather than being caught in the implementer's own loop.\",\"risk\":\"medium\",\"task\":\"task-06\",\"type\":\"decision\"}\ntests/fixtures/prose-and-position-journal.jsonl:59:{\"acceptance\":[\"TestSQLiteUnknownLanguageResponsesDoNotLeakAndPrimerRerenders passes\",\"Post-primer positive control still fails when language reveal is broken, verified by control-disable\",\"Pre-primer non-containment check is not vacuous, verified by making the response leak the client-visible title\",\"New assertion proves no raw entity:-style storage-ID token reaches a client-visible render payload and fails if sanitisation is bypassed\",\"Diff touches test files only; no renderer or production file modified\",\"gates.sh full passes including the whole internal/world/sqlite suite\"],\"files\":[\"internal/world/sqlite/languages_test.go\"],\"goal\":\"Correct TestSQLiteUnknownLanguageResponsesDoNotLeakAndPrimerRerenders, which pins a pre-existing storage-ID leak as expected behaviour (check-26, bd palimpsest-0pp). Express the language-reveal contract in terms of the client-visible resolved title, fix the pre-primer non-containment check so it does not become vacuous, and add a standing guard that no raw entity: token reaches a client-visible render payload. Test files only.\",\"id\":\"task-07\",\"status\":\"pending\",\"type\":\"task\"}\ntests/fixtures/prose-and-position-journal.jsonl:80:{\"basis\":[],\"finding\":\"The pre-close gates validation surfaced two orchestrator journal-hygiene defects, both of which are permanent under append-only journaling and will therefore appear as recorded residual issues in the close payload rather than being cleaned away. First, every execution_result in this run was written with status \\\"completed\\\", while the schema's terminal set is {complete, blocked, failed}. Second, four Gate 3 verdict citations were written into verification evidence[] as repository-relative paths, but the validator resolves evidence entries against the run directory via resolve_run_path, so they are reported as missing files even though all four exist unchanged on disk.\",\"id\":\"decision-15\",\"outcome\":\"claude_decision\",\"resolution\":\"Corrected by appending, never by rewriting (FR-120), and the residue is disclosed rather than hidden. For the status defect, four corrected execution_result records were appended carrying status \\\"complete\\\" with each original observation preserved verbatim; the original malformed lines remain and stay flagged, which is the intended cost of an append-only journal. For the evidence-path defect, the formal citation-correction mechanism does NOT apply: _citation_targets only builds targets from path tokens inside a verification's observation text or a decision's basis array, so an attempt to correct evidence[] entries was correctly refused with 'citation correction target does not exist'. The corrected run-relative paths are therefore recorded here in prose for any reader of the archive: check-12 evidence[0] should read claude-review-final-t03/verdict.md; check-15 evidence[0] claude-review-final-t03/merge-verdict.md; check-30 evidence[0] claude-review-final-prose/verdict.md; check-31 evidence[0] claude-review-final-prose/merge-verdict.md. All four files exist and were verified non-empty (10702, 10008, 16937 and 11411 bytes). One evidence artifact is genuinely lost rather than mis-cited: check-14 cites .forge/tmp/mutation-evidence-8c131667...log, which was written inside the codex-impl-01 worktree's .forge/tmp and destroyed when that worktree was removed after reintegration; its full content is quoted verbatim inside check-14's own observation, so the evidence survives in the journal even though the artifact does not. Practice for future runs: write mutation and gate evidence under the run directory, cite evidence[] run-relative, and use status \\\"complete\\\".\",\"risk\":\"low\",\"task\":\"task-07\",\"type\":\"decision\"}\ntests/test_drift_check.py:226:        parsed = json.loads(result.stdout)\ntests/test_drift_check.py:29