on, so the suite only ever exercises up to 9, and the implementer's own prose_cache_test.go applies 0010 explicitly rather than through ApplySave. Root cause is an orchestrator scoping error, not implementer negligence: the task-04 assignment granted internal/world/migrations/save/0010_prose_cache.sql but never internal/world/migrations/migrations.go, so wiring the migration was outside its stated ownership and it correctly reported the gap instead of editing an unowned file. Remediation: focused fix execution bumping SaveVersion to 10 with a test that fails if a save migration file exists above SaveVersion.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"git status --short internal/world/dbgen; git diff internal/world/queries/save.sql\", \"criterion\": \"gate-2: generated code drift \\u2014 make sqlcgen then git diff --exit-code internal/world/dbgen must be clean\", \"id\": \"check-17\", \"observation\": \"CONFIRMED unmet. internal/world/queries/save.sql changed materially \\u2014 ListRenderedTexts now inner-joins document_opens, DeleteRenderedTexts became conditional on NOT EXISTS in document_opens, and three queries are new (ListRenderedTextCaches, ListDocumentOpens, InsertDocumentOpen with ON CONFLICT (copy_id) DO NOTHING) \\u2014 while internal/world/dbgen shows no modification at all. sqlc-generated bindings for the new and changed queries are therefore absent, so the drift gate cannot pass and the sqlite layer cannot be compiling against regenerated bindings. The implementer was blocked from make sqlcgen by network restrictions on the pinned sqlc module and disclosed this plainly. Remediation: orchestrator or fix execution runs make sqlcgen (the orchestrator has proven network access for pinned module downloads \\u2014 make typegen succeeded earlier in this run) and then requires git diff --exit-code internal/world/dbgen to be clean.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-02 && go test -count=1 -timeout 60m ./internal/world/sqlite\", \"criterion\": \"gate-1: project tests \\u2014 full internal/world/sqlite suite on the task-04 candidate\", \"id\": \"check-18\", \"observation\": \"REAL DEFECT, not the known timeout hazard: FAIL palimpsest/internal/world/sqlite after 1966.630s, well inside the 60m budget, with assertion failures rather than a timeout panic, so bd palimpsest-6c3 is not implicated. TestEveryGeneratedCommissionIsDeskReachableAcrossSeeds failed on seed-42 and seed-2 with 'build prose render \\\"copy:p5:0000003\\\": work \\\"work:p5:000002\\\" date label: language \\\"language:p2:0001\\\" has no calendar', then 'walked 0 commissions across five seeds, want reviewed 30'. Root cause traced by the orchestrator: internal/prose deliberately REFUSES to render without a composition date label (render.go:29, pinned by TestRenderRequiresExplicitCompositionDateLabel) so that prose can never invent a date; the sqlite adapter builds that label in compositionDateLabel (renders.go:575-589) from a languages-to-calendars join on culture_id (renders.go:451-455), and returns a hard error when a language's culture has no calendar row. Because the prose prewarm is eager, that error propagates and a SINGLE dateless work makes the whole save fail to open \\u2014 blast radius is total even though prevalence is partial. Prevalence measured, not assumed: all 16 cached generated worlds under ~/.cache/palimpsest/test-worlds have every language calendared (m2-era worlds have no calendars table at all), yet 2 of the 5 freshly generated seeds in this test hit a dateless language, so the condition is seed-dependent and neither universal nor rare. Targeted prose tests could not catch this because their fixtures always supply a DateLabel. Other gates on the same candidate all passed: go build 0, go vet 0, architecturelint PASS, make sqlcgen idempotent with dbgen regenerated, internal/prose PASS, internal/world/migrations PASS, internal/world/savecanonical PASS, internal/sim/documents PASS 47.041s (diag-1 unchanged), internal/world/fixture PASS 5.872s, make save-robustness PASS 180.323s.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-02 && go test -count=1 -timeout 60m ./internal/world/sqlite (FR-121 pass 1 and 2)\", \"criterion\": \"Second internal/world/sqlite failure after the dateline fix: NULL scan on present_copy_state.terminal_event_id\", \"id\": \"check-19\", \"observation\": \"The dateline fix WORKED \\u2014 the 'has no calendar' failures are gone from both passes \\u2014 but two further failures were exposed behind it, so both FR-121 passes still failed (2169.177s and 1555.808s; both completed well inside the 60m budget with assertion failures, so bd palimpsest-6c3 is again not implicated). Failure A, now fixed: renders_prose_test.go:479 and :527 (TestPinnedLegacyCacheSkipsSupportedRerender) reported 'list present copy states for render cache: sql: Scan error on column index 5, name \\\"terminal_event_id\\\": converting NULL to string is unsupported'. Diagnosed as a TEST-FIXTURE defect, not a production defect: the real world schema declares present_copy_state.terminal_event_id as TEXT NOT NULL (migrations/world/0006_custody_decay.sql:46) and the pre-existing sqlc scan therefore targets a plain string, but the implementer's synthetic fixture declared the column nullable and inserted NULL, which production forbids. Fixed by tightening the fixture to match production (column declared NOT NULL, insert supplies 'event:test'), so the fixture can no longer drift laxer than the schema it stands in for. Also fixed in the same pass: a go vet regression the orchestrator itself introduced, 'internal/prose/acceptance_test.go:250: self-assignment of input.Work.ComposedToTick'; go vet ./... now exits 0.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-02 && go test -count=1 -run TestMissingRenderIsBackfilledWithDiagnosticRender ./internal/world/sqlite; read internal/prose/render.go:74-76, internal/prose/model.go:68-70, internal/world/sqlite/renders.go:528-530\", \"criterion\": \"P-PROJ-1: prose anchors must match the passage IDs disclosure and extraction key on\", \"id\": \"check-20\", \"observation\": \"LATENT DISCLOSURE DEFECT found and fixed \\u2014 more serious than the test failure that exposed it. conformance_test.go:100 reported 'claim \\\"claim:ledger-pins-intact\\\" passages [\\\"passage:ledger:pins\\\"], want exactly [\\\"passage:claim:ledger-pins-intact\\\"]'. The candidate hard-coded the assumption that a claim's anchor is always the derived form: internal/prose/render.go:74 computed passageID as \\\"passage:\\\"+claim.ID and rejected any supplied value that differed, model.go:68-70 documented that as the contract, and internal/world/sqlite/renders.go:528-530 refused any recorded passage list that was not exactly the derived singleton. The assumption is false: claims.passage_ids is authored data and real anchors are arbitrary \\u2014 the Halwen exemplar uses passage:muster:watch and this fixture uses passage:ledger:pins. The consequence is not merely a rejected render. The disclosure projection replaces runs by matching the claim's RECORDED passage ID (internal/world/fixture/skills_runtime.go:280-315), so had the derived form been emitted for an authored claim, the anchored factual run would not have been matched by redaction and masked claim text would have reached the client \\u2014 precisely this repository's dominant recurring defect class. Fixed so the recorded anchor is authoritative and is emitted verbatim, deriving only when the adapter supplies nothing; zero or multiple recorded passages remain an error because prose emits exactly one factual run per claim and the anchor would otherwise be ambiguous. Pinned by a new TestRenderEmitsSuppliedPassageAnchorVerbatim which asserts an authored anchor survives, that the derived form does NOT appear, and that an absent anchor still derives, with a guard that fails if the fixture anchor coincides with the derived form. The implementer's TestPassageIDMustExactlyMatchClaimID pinned the old wrong contract and was deliberately superseded by TestRenderRejectsClaimWithoutID, retaining the half that remains valid.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-02 && go test -count=1 -run TestMissingRenderIsBackfilledWithDiagnosticRender ./internal/world/sqlite\", \"criterion\": \"Third internal/world/sqlite failure: prose refuses claim event types its phrase bank does not cover\", \"id\": \"check-21\", \"observation\": \"OPEN, not fixed by the orchestrator, and deliberately handed back for an implementer pass. With the anchor fix in place the same conformance test progresses further and then fails with 'format prose render \\\"copy:repair-ledger\\\": genre \\\"ledger\\\" does not support claim event type \\\"inspection\\\"'. This is the THIRD instance of one systemic fault rather than three unrelated bugs: the prose layer treats every condition it cannot render as a hard error (absent calendar in check-18, unsupported claim event type here, and by inspection also a missing bank role or unsupported genre), while ensureDiagnosticRenders prewarms EAGERLY on save open, so any single unrenderable document anywhere in the world makes the entire save fail to open. Patching each trigger individually cannot close the class, because the set of unrenderable conditions is open-ended: real worlds contain event types, genres and metadata combinations the shipped ledger/catalog banks do not cover. The structural answer is the fallback that decision-01 already specified but which the candidate never wired: any copy prose cannot render must silently fall back to the diag-1 renderer for that copy, leaving the save openable and the document readable, with the fallback observable and tested. Recorded as decision-10 and assigned to a fresh implementer.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-04\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-03 && go test -count=1 -timeout 60m ./internal/world/sqlite (x2) && make save-robustness\", \"criterion\": \"gate-1: project tests \\u2014 full sqlite suite and save-robustness on the task-05 fallback candidate (FR-122 INVALID MEASUREMENT, discarded)\", \"id\": \"check-22\", \"observation\": \"MEASUREMENT INVALID under FR-122 machine instability; discarded rather than attributed to the candidate. Observed: FR-121 pass 1 FAIL at exactly 3600.047s (the 60m timeout, with database/sql goroutines mid-flight), pass 2 FAIL at 3394.813s, and make save-robustness FAIL exit 2 despite having passed at 170.803s on the immediately preceding candidate. Root cause is environmental, not code: failures report 'insert beliefs 4000..5000: database or disk is full (13)' from generate_test.go:372 and testcache_test.go:112. Investigation found / has 464G free (4% of 503G) but /tmp is a 7.9G tmpfs that was 100% full with 50M available; Go's $WORK and SQLite's temp files resolve to TMPDIR, so both exhausted. This also retro-corrects an earlier orchestrator misdiagnosis in this session: a sandbox probe that failed with 'no space left on device' was attributed to a small sandbox tmpfs when the real cause was this same exhausted /tmp. Contributing factor identified: ~3.1G of abandoned Go build/mod caches in /tmp (codex-review-go-cache 870M, bku11-go-build-cache 750M, codex-review-gocache 358M, go-cache-review-3bx13 333M, codex-review-gomod 304M, two tenant-review caches 265M each), all 6-7 hours old, created by agents in OTHER sessions redirecting their Go caches into /tmp to escape the same read-only-cache sandbox restriction documented for this run's implementers \\u2014 so the sandbox policy problem materially caused the disk problem. Remediation applied: pruned only stale palimpsest/Test/go-build temp dirs older than 90 minutes (59 of 60 palimpsest dirs), reclaiming 4.2G and taking /tmp from 50M to 4.3G free, deliberately leaving other live sessions' named caches intact. Per FR-122 the affected verification re-runs from scratch with TMPDIR relocated to disk-backed storage; timings from this attempt are discarded and not used as evidence for or against the candidate.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-05\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-03 && TMPDIR=/home/agents/.cache/palimpsest-tmp go test -count=1 -timeout 60m ./internal/world/sqlite (x2)\", \"criterion\": \"gate-1: project tests \\u2014 full sqlite suite must pass on the fallback candidate\", \"id\": \"check-24\", \"observation\": \"ONE remaining failure, reproducible in both passes and fully isolated now that the disk noise is gone: TestLiveThreeWayReplayCrashAtEverySaveBoundary fails at boundaries migration-before-apply and migration-after-apply (6.98s and 6.93s) with 'reopen after migration-after-apply: incompatible save database: user_version is 9, want 10; choose a new --save path' (live_replay_crash_test.go:79). Root cause traced, and it is TEST SCAFFOLDING, not production code, and it belongs to the orchestrator's own SaveVersion bump (decision-08) rather than to the implementer's fallback work. downgradeCrashFixtureToV8 (live_replay_crash_test.go:157-175) is hard-coded to the v8-to-v9 world: it runs 'DROP TABLE economy_events; DELETE FROM schema_migrations WHERE version=9; PRAGMA user_version=8', undoing exactly migration 0009. Because the base fixture is now built at SaveVersion 10, its schema_migrations ledger still contains a row for version 10, so on reopen applyThrough re-applies 0009 (setting user_version 9) and then SKIPS 0010 via its already-recorded-checksum continue, leaving the save at 9 while validatePragmas demands 10. This is the same defect CLASS as check-16: version-coupled scaffolding that silently goes stale the moment a migration is added, and then fails in a way that looks like a production migration bug. Remediation must generalise the helper rather than retarget it at 10, so adding migration 0011 cannot reintroduce this; live_replay_crash_test.go is outside task-05's granted ownership, so it is assigned onward rather than patched here.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-05\"}, {\"check\": \"cd .worktrees/codex-impl-04 && .forge/tmp/bin/gates.sh full ; .forge/tmp/bin/gates.sh sqlite\", \"criterion\": \"gate-1: full sqlite suite after the crash-fixture generalisation (FR-121 both passes)\", \"id\": \"check-25\", \"observation\": \"check-24 is CLOSED: TestLiveThreeWayReplayCrashAtEverySaveBoundary now passes at every boundary (280.358s standalone) and the generalisation was verified independently by the orchestrator \\u2014 grep finds no hard-coded save version or migration-specific table left in the crash fixture, createReplaySaveBeforeLatestMigration builds it via the new migrations.ApplySaveThrough, and assertCrashFixtureNeedsMigration derives its expectation from migrations.SaveVersion-1 with a ledger-row check, so a future 0011 needs no edit. The crash-marker device also genuinely proves each boundary is reached rather than asserting it: the marker is written up front and removed by a deferred cleanup, and maybeCrash terminates without running defers, so only an actually-reached boundary leaves it behind. Everything else on the candidate passed in both runs: go build, go vet, architecturelint, prose+migrations+savecanonical, internal/world/fixture, internal/sim/documents 44s, make save-robustness 171s, make sqlcgen, and dbgen drift clean. ONE new failure, reproducible in both passes at 19.22s and 19.26s: TestSQLiteUnknownLanguageResponsesDoNotLeakAndPrimerRerenders, languages_test.go:215, post-primer positive control. Of its three clauses only 'readable.Metadata.Title != canonicalTitle' fails; LanguageReadable is true and the access key did change. See check-26 for the diagnosis, which reverses the apparent direction of fault.\", \"result\": \"failed\", \"task\": \"task-06\"}, {\"check\": \"No mutation tool available for go \\u2014 assertion-quality fallback only.\", \"criterion\": \"mutation: go\", \"id\": \"mutation-9d5d576e942848918bbf7438205887f1\", \"observation\": \"tool=none; scope=go; outcome=declared-absence; scoped_files=[\\\"internal/simprofile/profile_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/canonical/canonical_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/fixture/schema_v2_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/sqlite/generate_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/sqlite/live_foundations_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/sqlite/motivation_persistence_test.go\\\",\\\"internal/world/sqlite/testcache_test.go\\\"]; timeout=not-applicable\", \"result\": \"skipped\", \"task\": \"task-08\"}], \"ok\": false, \"profile\": \"gates\", \"warnings\": [\"line 107: legacy compatibility declaration active: records before this declaration span three sessions and multiple journal dialects (non-terminal execution_result statuses, string and since-removed-worktree evidence references, duplicate execution_result and decision ids, one non-canonical gate criterion); all substantive gates passed with recorded orchestrator-run evidence (checks 38-45) and history is append-only, so tolerance is declared per the palimpsest-bjb posture rather than rewriting history; strict schema applies after this line.\", \"execution codex-impl-05/execution-01 handoff is missing or empty\"]}\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:26:from tests._cli_loader import load_script, package_module, patch_app, patch_chain_core, patch_engine  # cli split phase 0: one shared loader\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:29:CLI = load_script(\"forge_cli_merge_adapter_tests\", CLI_PATH)\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:34:FIXTURE_SUPPORT = load_script(\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:358:        intent_digest = json.loads(\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:767:    def test_each_new_adapter_control_is_load_bearing(self) -> None:\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:824:        state = store.load(self.chain_id)\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:827:            json.loads(line)\ntests/test_cli_merge_adapters.py:837:     